l intermittent fasting  um however she does say that this is  kind of just like she does naturally  so it doesn't really feel like i guess  restrictive to her  um so if it works for her and it feels  good to her great it works for me having  said all of that  i'm still getting questions from a lot  of you in dms and messages  about whether or not this whole keto  reset concept  is healthy so it seems like this  video or these two videos have really  had a lasting effect on a lot of you  even after she's pulled them down so  because of that i do think it is  necessary for me to speak to  the concept of this keto diet reset and  whether or not  it is healthy um kind of helpful  and also even safe also a quick reminder  that i do really respect san for pulling  these videos  so big props to her for that so this  video that i shot a few months ago was  never really meant to be  a strict what i in a day review so i  really just wanted to speak to the  concept of the keto diet reset  as well as its intentions and its  impacts  so let's do it started doing like  a diet for one or two weeks where we're  researching which carbs will be good for  us  and we're determining element we're  doing kind of like an elimination diet  sorry i had to look at the word um where  we see where we have like certain  allergies or food sensitivities  so we started working out with this new  trainer and they were really  trying to find out also what caused  inflammation in his body  then for me i go i used to go to bed  at least a couple times a week with a  really  really painful stomach completely  bloated extremely  extremely gassy i have such  well-balanced meals right with my  protein carbs i've been always very on  top of that but i was  i felt that there was something in my  body what i was eating  what really really hurt and i was just  extremely gassy which is so  so uncomfortable i wanted to try this  out and see which foods actually  like work for us and which one those  okay so  according to her june 16th video why we  changed our diet  san and her partner are going on a  two-week body  reset with the intention of alleviating  things like  body pain muscle soreness inflammation  gas and bloating so this body reset  comes at the recommendation of their  trainer  who claims that going on a keto diet for  two weeks  and completely eliminating carbohydrates  is a guaranteed way to  fix these problems so this means for  them  only eating fats and proteins and some  vegetables  while cutting out carbs dairy eggs  sugar and nightshade veggies on top of  all of these restrictions san also  mentions that she is  intermittent fasting so yeah  that's a lot of rules but at the end of  these two weeks  they apparently will slowly reintroduce  carbohydrate-containing foods  and test their blood sugar levels to  assess their body's reaction to that  food  and whether or not it affects their  system in a good way or a  bad way there's a lot to unpack here  to start off apparently they're testing  their blood sugar to see if this  reset resolves their inflammation now  while blood tests  are often used to detect inflammation in  the body  these blood tests will specifically look  at  inflammatory markers like c-reactive  protein  plasma viscosity and erythrocyte  sedimentation rate  testing blood sugar levels would not be  considered an indicator of  inflammation blood sugar levels are  instead  an indicator of how efficient your body  is  at breaking down and absorbing  carbohydrates for fuel  so if the purpose of this reset is to  relieve inflammation  it doesn't really make any sense to me  that they're using their blood sugar  levels  as a gauge of success number two one of  the other reasons that sun is going on  an elimination diet  is to improve her digestive issues like  bloating  and gas now while elimination diets are  often recommended to help identify food  triggers for digestive issues  keto is not typically how it's done  research has found that the keto diet or  a very low carb diet  may be associated with relief from ibs  or other digestive disorders  but it's believed that this is not  because of ketosis per se  but because of a reduction in  fermentable carbohydrates  otherwise known as high fodmap foods  that just  tend to accompany a low carb regime  i also want to note that depending on  the true cause of the digestive issue  the keto diet may actually make things  worse  one of the major complaints about keto  is the risk of constipation due to a  lack of fiber  and there is some early research to  suggest that it may even cause  inflammation  and reduce the concentration of good  bacteria in the gut  so obviously we need more research in  this area  but until then keto would not be the  first step i would suggest  most digestion rds would plan a proper  low fodmap elimination diet  which would involve removing foods that  are high in fodmaps and then gradually  reintroducing them back in varying  amounts it's not meant to be a  reset and it's not meant to be a  lifelong diet either  it's a carefully controlled test that  should be overseen  by a dietitian not a trainer for whom  therapeutic diets  like this is really out of their scope  as for keto and inflammation  there was some research suggesting that  a very low carb diet  may help reduce inflammation in those  with type 2 diabetes  however they also found that there were  just naturally  higher rates of inflammation in people  with insulin resistance so it's unclear  how this can be applied to the general  population not to mention that the  impact was dependent on the  type of carbohydrate with refined or  simple carbs  having the biggest inflammatory effect  now nightshade veggies like tomatoes  eggplants potatoes and peppers  are often thought to be pro-inflammatory  specifically in the context of arthritis  and this is due to their alkaloid  content  specifically the alkaloid solanine there  is also some animal and test tube  research suggesting that nightshade  veggies  may increase inflammation but according  to the arthritis foundation  this is largely a myth in fact  a 2011 study found that both  inflammation  and arthritis was actually reduced in  healthy men who ate nightshades for six  weeks  and then another 2013 study found that a  specific alkaloid  found in nightshades called anitabine  had  powerful anti-inflammatory effects of  course research is not anecdotal  experience so  some people may find relief from cutting  out a variety of things  i'm not discounting anyone's experience  here  but this is why it's important to really  work with a dietitian  to properly identify unique triggers  specific to you and finally  sam mentions that by following this diet  she'll be eating  a lot of meat which is definitely  something that it doesn't seem like  she's  used to and as we've seen in her  previous videos  she tends to have a preference towards  eating more plant-based foods so  this will definitely be a change of pace  but if the purpose of this diet is to  reduce  inflammation eating larger quantities of  meat on a more frequent basis  probably wouldn't be my first  recommendation  as research shows that higher red meat  intakes  are associated with increased risk of  inflammation  now with all of that said i feel like  the focus of this diet  is just to remove foods that may be  allegedly inflammatory  which doesn't leave us with a whole lot  i believe it may be just as important if  not more important  to add foods in that are  anti-inflammatory  so this includes foods with omega-3 fats  like fatty fish  olive oil walnuts and flax seed all of  which  are keto friendly options foods that are  high in antioxidants are  also anti-inflammatory and they often  include foods that are  not so much keto friendly like fruit and  some starchy vegetables  honestly i'm just getting the impression  that the term body reset  is like a synonym for detox as  sun is using a lot of the same rhetoric  we heard her use in her detox video  so unfortunately we're already off to a  not so great start  and one that doesn't make a whole lot of  sense to begin with  based on basic human physiology  but let's take a look at some of sans  meals and warning again  i'm gonna talk numbers so since i can't  necessarily show her meals  i am cutting out any of my in-depth  commentary on these meals just to be  fair  and like i said this video was never  really meant to be a strict wedding day  review anyways it's more about  the concept of the keto diet reset  however  just to give you guys some context and  an understanding of  some of her recommendations that her  trainer was giving her  i want to share my rough thoughts and my  rough notes on  what she was eating that day so sam's  menu included  some bulletproof coffee a salad with  lots of veggies greens avocado olives  and oil  fried brussels sprouts in oil with  avocado and a chocolate pudding made of  avocado  cocoa powder almond butter oil and  protein powder  so overall when we look at sam's meals  for the day  she's getting around 95 grams of  carbohydrates  which makes up around 23 of her calories  from carbs as i mentioned the keto diet  recommendation for carbs  is usually less than around 50 grams in  order to stand ketosis  which would be between five to ten  percent  of total calories from carbs for the day  i have to be honest  i'm just not really sure what the goal  is here  is it ketosis reducing bloating and  inflammation  just eating a healthy low carb and  higher fat plant-based diet  it seems like none of these goals are  being met adequately  because sans trainer has her tackling  way too much at one time  and none of it seems actually evidence  based  or aligned with sun's personal  preferences but anyways  let's move on to part two now in her  follow-up video entitled a model trice  elimination diet and results  son talks about the hate that she got  from her first video  and that she was simply sharing her  journey and not suggesting that anyone  else follow suit  now i really always appreciate  influencers putting out disclaimers like  this  because some people will hopefully take  it with a grain of salt  but there will always be a huge  proportion of her followers  who will watch this and think well if a  model is doing this extreme diet  and i am bloating too i should do it too  as  influencers we have a huge amount of  responsibility for  what we put out there and there was a  lot of  misinformation that clearly wasn't fact  checked before it went out case in point  this right here  this week we will be doing a carb test  thing  which means we're gonna take our blood  sugar levels before and after we  eat carbs and we're gonna see what it  does with a spike in her body  so if it spikes a lot i mean i'm i'm  using really basic terms right now not a  nutritionist at all if it spikes a lot  your body changes a carbohydrate  immediately into like insulin and  fat and if it doesn't spike that much  your body use really the carbohydrate  for energy  well first of all again i don't know  what blood sugar testing has to do with  inflammation and bloating  we have a lot of groundwork to cover  here now we typically see people with  diabetes checking their blood sugars  before and after a meal  to evaluate the effect of that meal on  their corresponding blood sugar levels  or to determine how much insulin they  may need  but again it doesn't really have  anything to do with inflammation  and if you don't have diabetes testing  your blood sugar levels before and after  you eat  is unnecessary because your insulin  functions  optimally enough to bring blood sugar  levels back down  into the normal range it doesn't matter  if you're eating  low carb or high carb if you are healthy  your blood sugars will stay within a  normal range i'm also a bit concerned  about sans  understanding of carbohydrates blood  sugar levels  insulin and how they all relate to one  another  so to clarify for all of you when we  consume a carbohydrate containing food  the body breaks it down into glucose  which is used for energy  the body does not break down  carbohydrates into insulin  as sand describes insulin is actually  the hormone that's released from the  body and helps the body get glucose  into the cells so that it can be used  for energy insulin is released  from the body every time we eat  carbohydrates  regardless of the effect that it has on  blood sugar levels  the only time that carbohydrates get  converted into fat  is if more glucose is consumed than what  can be comfortably stored as glycogen  which is the storage form of glucose so  with all this in mind  i can understand why sun would think  that a high blood sugar spike  might mean that the carbohydrate would  be used as fat  but that's not necessarily the case  eating one high glycemic index food  like white rice for example does not  mean that it will be immediately  converted into fat  it really has more to do with an overall  pattern of eating  and not about one type of food eaten at  one  point in time also in general we don't  usually eat carbohydrate foods  in isolation we often combine foods like  white rice  with a source of protein fat and some  fibrous veggies  all of which can help to slow down the  rate at which glucose is broken down  in the body and used for energy bottom  line  sans trainer is peddling some serious  faulty  science let's hear a little bit more now  for one week  i have zero complaints with my stomach i  wake up  like it's super flat it's not even about  that it just feels very good i feel  lighter  i think in that way it's been a really  good week  on the other side though i love food so  much and i really miss  like eggs and like nuts and all these  things because you would be surprised  like how many things you cannot eat with  this  so for me every time frying veggies with  like a protein it's just it gets boring  especially around like lunch time  because we're also intermitting fasting  i just want to have like breakfast i  want oats or i want like a smoothie or  something and we can't really have that  right now i  do feel really good which is a good  thing i find  food very boring right now okay so sam  says that she's feeling pretty good on  this diet which is great to hear  though i don't love that she describes  her digestive relief in terms of being  super flat  and lighter but whatever minor point  the bigger issue is that this relief  comes at the cost of her saying that  she's  bored with foods that she's allowed to  eat and misses  a lot of her go-to's like eggs smoothies  oatmeal and nuts if you were to ask me  i would say that this bogus combination  of restrictions  and faux keto is not the reason  for her relief but rather that she's  reduced a lot  of high fodmap carbs so since she went  to the extreme of cutting out carbs  even low fodmap carbs it does make sense  that she's  feeling pretty good and it's unfortunate  that she did this whole  non-evidence-based roundabout way of a  low  fodmap trial because a it wasn't even  done properly as i did pick up on a few  higher fodmap foods in her day like  mushrooms and brussels sprouts  and b there were so many  foods that were cut out unnecessarily at  this point  this diet trial or reset has told sun  nothing specific about the actual unique  foods  causing her grief and in order to do  this properly  she's going to have to start again under  the supervision of a dietitian  who can walk her through the careful  steps  of a properly planned elimination diet  the goal  is not to remove fodmaps or groups of  foods for life  the goal is to test each subclass and  also at different amounts to come to  conclusions on  which are actually issues for her  let's see what her carb testing revealed  yesterday  i did rise and went up from 74 to 160  guys  and i love white rice  so obviously you're gonna ask me does  this mean i'm never gonna eat rice again  no absolutely not i love it  um i love sushi too but it's just for me  good to know that  whenever i really want to build up the  muscle and like get dry  that i should probably not eat rice but  try something else  so yeah okay so san tested white rice  and found that her blood sugar went up  from 74 to 160.  um i'm not really surprised  white rice is classified as a high  glycemic index food meaning  it increases blood sugar faster than  foods with more fiber  that provide a more steady stream of  energy  it's important to know however that  eating rice in combination with  other foods would not have had the same  impact on blood sugar levels  which is actually really important thing  to keep in mind this is why i always  talk so much about aiming for  higher fiber carbohydrates and pairing  them with a source of protein  or fat aka not eating naked carbs  whenever you can and most of us do this  naturally because we have sushi  or stir-fry or rice and beans etc  most of us don't have mono meals of just  one  single ingredient i'm also glad to hear  that san  doesn't intend to stop eating rice  because of some of these  results because rice is something that  she actually just really really loves  however she also said that this  information is good to know  because now she knows not to eat rice  whenever she wants to  build muscle and get dry so  not only is there some obvious confusion  and misinformation  around the relationship between  carbohydrates and insulin and blood  sugar levels  but also around muscle building and  carbs as well  getting dry and building muscles are two  very different goals each with two very  different sports nutrition protocols  to build muscles obviously you need some  kind of  loaded stimulus like lifting weights  plus  you'll need enough protein to rebuild  and maintain those muscles  carbohydrates even high glycemic index  carbs  are actually helpful in this situation  because a  they help give you the energy to perform  this work and  b they elicit an insulin response to  help drive the protein  into the muscle faster now getting dry  is actually not a healthy state period  this is something that people do just to  get stage ready for like a fitness  competition  and is unfortunately a look that people  strive for because they stare at  instagram models all day  but getting dry for a fitness  competition may involve  limiting water and limiting carbs to dry  out your glycogen stores  and then right before your show you  might be instructed to  carb load to fill your glycogen stores  just enough that your muscles look  pronounced and full but not too much  that they spill over into the space  between  your muscle and your skin and then you  look what they call  watery so i just want to clear up that  these are two  very different goals with two very  different ways of achieving them  with the latter being a potentially  dangerous one  for the general population again all of  this test really tells us  is that eating rice in isolation of  other foods  will spike your blood sugar levels which  is something  we already know for everyone  that is normal and healthy and unless  you have diabetes  is not actually a major issue other than  the fact that  maybe it leaves you hungry soon after  because it lacks  staying power let's see some of her  conclusions from the experiment really  a lot for me also like white rice but  does that mean that i  never can eat that no it doesn't it just  means  that when i want to eat carbs for energy  instead of fat  i should eat um chickpeas brown rice and  like sourdough bread  and we're going to obviously talk to a  doctor about all of this but  it actually does make sense and just as  a cool experiment it was fun to do  okay so this is a good little takeaway  from this experiment  of course carbs that are lower in fiber  fat or protein  are going to have a greater impact on  blood sugar levels  than those that are packaged up with  some of these state-shading compounds  like chickpeas or higher fiber bread  brown rice  etc i mean she could have also skipped  the whole keto diet thing  and just looked up the glycemic index  chart online  but i also totally get that some people  really like that personalized experience  and data collection and that's great  again the glycemic index of individual  carb foods isn't really that important  because we tend to eat them with sources  of protein  or sources of fat which will completely  alter  the overall glycemic load but bottom  line  i fully get that a lot of people want to  get to the bottom of their unpleasant  symptoms  like bloating gas and inflammation which  is a  kind of vague ambiguous term to begin  with to be honest  and yes a proper elimination diet would  be the best way to achieve this  that pursuit is not my issue here my  issue  is that the diet and the methodology of  testing its effectiveness  did not complement the goals here at all  and was really not evidence-based which  is something that an  actual dietitian would be really careful  to consider  so a testing blood sugars to come to the  conclusion that brown rice  has a less aggressive effect on blood  sugars compared to white rice  does not tell us anything about bloating  or gas  or inflammation and actually sam didn't  even comment on the impact  of this test on inflammation whatever  that means to her  b going pseudo keto plus removing  nightshades  may be like a roundabout way to reduce  bloating depending on the cause  but it certainly added a lot of  restrictions and is  not as complete as a low fodmap trial  and c testing blood sugars if you don't  have diabetes  or insulin resistance doesn't tell us  anything about how well  a reset worked because blood sugars will  always be regulated within a healthy  range period  this diet sounds like a legitimate hell  and just not compatible with sans diet  or lifestyle especially in combination  with intermittent fasting that she  already does it's also unnecessarily  restrictive  and will probably need to be redone in  order for son to get  any actual answers on her symptoms this  my friends is why you want to work with  a qualified  nutrition professional for making any  changes to your diet  because at best it can be a few brutal  weeks of unnecessary discomfort that  does not bring you  any kind of long-term relief and at  worst  it can be actually dangerous for your  physical and mental health  ultimately i really hope that san is  able to move on from this  reset experiment and find a way of  eating that is sustainable for her  and also helps give her the relief that  she seeks well folks that is all for  today's video  and again a big thank you again to  hellofresh for sponsoring  if you like this video please be sure to  give it the thumbs up  leave me a comment below with who you'd  like to see me review next until then  subscribe to the channel  and i'll see you next time on abby's  kitchen bye  you
 

